# Algo PBX reverse proxy — SAFE DEFAULT / TEMPLATE, not the file Caddy
# actually reads at runtime.
#
# Real bug found deploying this (2026-08-27): the original version of
# this file unconditionally declared `https://{$VM_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}` with a
# hard Cloudflare DNS-01 `tls` block. Caddy treats a failed automation-
# policy provisioning (e.g. CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN empty — the state every
# fresh deploy starts in, before an admin ever visits Settings) as a
# FATAL config-load error, not a per-site failure — the whole process
# exited and crash-looped, taking the web UI down entirely rather than
# degrading gracefully. That defeats "web:3000 stays independently
# reachable" (still true) AND "the site loads over plain HTTP at least"
# (was NOT true until this fix).
#
# The fix: this file is now a genuinely safe plain-HTTP-only config that
# ALWAYS parses and serves, and is never itself the mounted file —
# docker-compose.yml's `caddy` service mounts
# pbx_configs/generated/Caddyfile instead (gitignored, freely rewritable
# at deploy time, same pattern as pjsip_dynamic.conf/caddy.env).
# scripts/render-caddy-env.sh seeds that generated file from THIS one on
# first bring-up (so a fresh deploy needs no admin action to serve
# something); POST /api/admin/settings/domain/apply overwrites it with
# the full HTTPS + Cloudflare DNS-01 version (see that route) once a real
# domain + token are configured, then asks `cert-sync` to recreate the
# container.
#
# DNS-01 (not Caddy's default HTTP-01) was still the right call once a
# real domain exists — it needs no inbound port 80 reachability at all,
# works the same whether this VM is directly internet-facing or behind
# additional NAT/firewalling. See the generated version's own header
# (written by the apply route) for the rest of that reasoning, and this
# repo's git history for the pre-Loop-C4 manual-certbot version if this
# ever needs a full revert.

:80 {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
